      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nUCF's top three players are **Riley Kugel** (G, 6-5, Sr), **Jamichael Stillwell** (F, 6-8, Sr), and **Themus Fulks** (G, 6-2, Sr). Kugel leads the team at 17.2 PPG, dangerous with his explosive athleticism, pull-up jumpers, and transition scoring from his Mississippi State transfer experience[1][2]. Stillwell averages 14.8 PPG and 8.2 RPG, excelling as a physical bully-ball forward who dominates post mismatches and crashes the glass relentlessly[2][3]. Fulks chips in 12.5 PPG and 5.1 APG, a crafty point guard whose quickness, mid-range game, and playmaking create chaos in pick-and-roll sets[1][5].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nUCF plays at a **moderately up-tempo pace** (68.5 possessions per game), emphasizing **pack-line defensive principles** under coach Johnny Dawkins to force turnovers (18.2% opponent TO rate) and limit paint scoring. Offensively, they run a **gap-drive-and-kick scheme** with heavy reliance on **three-point volume** (38.2% of shots, 35.1% accuracy), supported by strong **offensive rebounding** (33.1% rate) from their size advantage with multiple 6-11+ bigs like Jeremy Foumena and Elijah Hulsewe[1][2].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), UCF went 7-3, including a four-game winning streak capped by a **Big 12 Tournament quarterfinals appearance** where they upset #6 Oklahoma 78-72 before falling to #2 Kansas 85-71 in the semis. Key wins: 82-76 at West Virginia (Kugel 22 pts) and 91-84 vs Baylor (Stillwell 18 pts, 12 reb)[3].\n\n## Strengths\n\nUCF excels in **size and physicality**, winning the rebounding battle in 75% of wins and forcing 15+ turnovers per game against guard-heavy foes. They thrive in **matchups vs smaller, perimeter-oriented teams** like those reliant on guard scoring, using their frontcourt (Hulsewe, Foumena, John Bol at 7-2) to clog lanes and convert second-chance points (14.2 per game)[2][5].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **elite perimeter shooting and switchable defenses**, as their guards (Kugel, Fulks) struggle against length (opponents shoot 37.8% from three in losses). **Slow half-court execution** against disciplined packs exposes them to zone schemes, and they rank outside Big 12 top-6 in free-throw defense (opponents 72.4% FT)[1][3].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full rotation healthy entering the East Region. Coach **Johnny Dawkins** brings proven tournament experience (Duke assistant on three national titles, Stanford Sweet 16 runs, UCF's 2024 NIT semis). UCF's March Madness history is limited (2 NCAA appearances, 0-2 record, last in 2016), but their 21-11 mark and Big 12 finish position them for a potential first-round upset[4].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Riley Kugel's three-point shooting**—if he heats up (career 34.2% but 41% in UCF wins), UCF's spacing unlocks drives and overwhelms #7 seeds; cold nights (under 30%) doom their offense[1][2].",
