      "perplexityContext": "## North Dakota State Bison Scouting Report: #14 Seed, East Region (2026 NCAA Tournament)\n\nNorth Dakota State enters the 2026 NCAA Tournament with a 24-7 record, strong **adjusted offensive efficiency of 112.8** and **defensive efficiency of 101.0**, positioning them as a dangerous mid-major upset threat in the East region.[2][4]\n\n## Key Players\n- **Trevian Carson (G, 6'3\" Jr.)**: Averages 17.2 PPG, leads the team in scoring with explosive drives and 38% three-point shooting (4.1 makes/game). Dangerous as a quick first-step creator who exploits switches in pick-and-roll.[2][4]\n- **Noah Feddersen (F, 6'10\" Jr.)**: Posts 14.8 PPG and 8.2 RPG, dominating with post moves and 55% field goal efficiency. His length and rebounding make him a mismatch nightmare against smaller fronts.[2][4]\n- **Markhi Strickland (G, 6'6\" Sr.)**: Contributes 12.5 PPG and 5.1 APG, elite at 42% from three (3.8 makes/game) off screens. Deadly spot-up shooter who stretches defenses and punishes closeouts.[2][4]\n\n## Team Identity\nNorth Dakota State plays at a **deliberate tempo (64 possessions/game)**, emphasizing **pack-line defensive philosophy** that forces turnovers (18% opponent TO rate) and limits paint scoring. Offensively, they run a **gap-attack scheme** with high-low actions involving Feddersen, relying on **39% three-point shooting** (team-high 12.2 attempts/game at 39%) and **elite rebounding strength** (38.2 RPG, No. 1 in Summit League).[2][4]\n\n## Recent Form\nOver the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), NDSU went 8-2, including a **five-game winning streak** to close the regular season. They won the Summit League Tournament title, beating Oral Roberts 78-65 in the final and South Dakota State 71-68 in semis, outrebounding foes by +9 per game in tournament play.[2]\n\n## Strengths\nThey excel in **defensive rebounding (27.1 DRPG)** and forcing misses (49% opponent eFG%), ideal against teams lacking size. Matchups favor them vs. inefficient mid-majors or squads weak on the glass, where their efficiency edges (AdjEM +11.8) shine in grind-it-out games.[2][4]\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable to **high-tempo transition attacks** (they allow 1.18 PPP in fast breaks) and athletic wings who blow by guards. Perimeter-oriented teams with multiple 40%+ three-point shooters trouble them, as seen in two losses to Denver (both shot 41% from deep).[2]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported; full rotation healthy entering the dance. Coach **David Richman** has solid tournament experience (3-4 NCAA record, including 2023 First Round upset win). Program history includes 8 NCAA appearances, best run a 2014 Second Round berth after beating Oklahoma.[2][4]\n\n## X-Factor\n**Trevian Carson's ability to handle ball pressure**—if he limits turnovers (currently 2.8/game) against press defenses, NDSU can control tempo and upset a #3 seed; otherwise, they get run out early.[2]",
