      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Xaivian Lee (Sr. G, 6'4\", 18.2 PPG)**: Transferred from Princeton, Lee is an electric play-maker who thrives off the bounce, averaging 18.2 points, 4.1 assists, and 1.8 steals per game. His quick first step and ability to score at all three levels make him dangerous in transition and isolation[1][2][5].\n\n**Boogie Fland (So. G, 6'3\", 16.7 PPG)**: A sophomore transfer from Arkansas, Fland leads the backcourt with explosive athleticism, posting 16.7 points, 5.2 rebounds, and 3.9 assists. His pull-up shooting (38% from three) and elite finishing at the rim exploit defenses in pick-and-roll sets[2][3][5].\n\n**Micah Handlogten (Sr. C, 7'1\", 12.4 PPG, 9.1 RPG)**: The senior center anchors the paint with 12.4 points and 9.1 rebounds (4.2 offensive), shooting 62% from the field. His rebounding tenacity and screen-setting create mismatches for Florida's guards[2][3].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nFlorida plays at a high tempo (72.3 possessions per game), pushing the pace in transition where their athletic guards excel. Defensively, they employ a switch-heavy 1-3-1 zone philosophy under Todd Golden, ranking top-15 nationally in forcing turnovers (18.2% TO rate) while protecting the rim with length. Offensively, they run a motion-based scheme emphasizing ball movement (68 assists per 100 possessions) and three-point volume (36.8 attempts per game at 37.2%). Rebounding is a strength, led by Handlogten and Rueben Chinyelu (6'10\" Jr. C), with a +5.8 rebound margin.\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (Feb 17 - Mar 17), Florida went 9-2, including a seven-game winning streak capped by an SEC Tournament championship win over Alabama (78-72) on March 15. They averaged 82.1 points per game in that stretch, holding opponents to 66.4, with standout wins over Tennessee (85-76) and Kentucky (71-68). Their only losses were a neutral-site slip to Gonzaga (74-70) and a home quad-1 defeat to Auburn (69-65).\n\n## Strengths\n\nFlorida excels in **guard play and transition scoring**, leveraging Lee's and Fland's speed to generate 22.4 fast-break points per game. Their **defensive versatility** disrupts slower offenses, and elite rebounding (+5.8 margin) fuels second-chance points (14.2 per game). They favor matchups against mid-major or inefficient-shooting teams (sub-105 AdjO), as seen in SEC dominance over Vanderbilt and Missouri, where their length overwhelmed smaller lineups.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerabilities include **perimeter defense against elite shooters**, allowing 36.1% from three to top opponents, and occasional **turnover issues** (16.4% in losses). Physical, half-court teams with strong interior scoring—like Auburn or potential tournament foes like Houston—give them trouble by slowing tempo and exploiting Handlogten's foul-prone play (4.1 fouls per 40 minutes).\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; Handlogten is fully healthy after a minor ankle tweak in February. Coach Todd Golden brings solid tournament experience, reaching the Sweet 16 in 2024 and Elite Eight in 2025. Florida's program history includes two national titles (2006-07), six Final Fours, and consistent March success (23-12 since 2020)",
