      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Tamin Lipsey (Sr. G, 6'1\", 200 lbs)** averages 17.2 points, 4.1 assists, and 2.3 steals per game, making him dangerous with his elite perimeter defense, quickness in transition, and ability to break down defenders off the dribble.[1][2][4]  \n**Milan Momcilovic (Jr. F, 6'8\", 225 lbs)** scores 15.8 points and grabs 6.2 rebounds, excelling as a versatile wing who stretches the floor with 38% three-point shooting and attacks closeouts effectively.[1][2][5]  \n**Joshua Jefferson (Sr. F, 6'9\", 240 lbs)** contributes 13.4 points and 8.1 rebounds, posing matchup problems with his physicality, rim protection (1.2 blocks), and mid-range pull-up game.[1][2][4]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nIowa State plays at a deliberate tempo (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing a **no-middle defensive philosophy** under T.J. Otzelberger that funnels drivers into help defenders, ranking top-5 nationally in turnover-forcing rate (24.1%). Offensively, they run a cut-heavy scheme with high-low actions involving bigs like Jefferson and Mulder, relying on **elite three-point volume** (39.2% on 22.4 attempts) but ranking mid-tier in rebounding due to frontcourt youth.[1][5]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 17-March 17), Iowa State went 9-2, including a five-game winning streak capped by a **Big 12 Tournament semifinal win over Kansas (82-76) before falling to Houston in the final (71-68)**. Key wins: road at Baylor (78-72), home vs. Texas Tech (91-65).[5]\n\n## Strengths\n\nThey dominate with the **best defensive efficiency in the nation (94.2 adj.)**, suffocating opponents in the half-court and thriving against guard-heavy or inefficient-shooting teams. Matchups favor them vs. slower-paced Big 12 foes or squads lacking size to counter Jefferson/Mulder inside-out play.[1][5]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to explosive transition attacks and teams with superior athleticism at the wing, as their deliberate style struggles against high-tempo offenses (lost to fast-paced Oklahoma twice). Poor offensive rebounding (28.4% rate) exposes them to second-chance points from physical bigs.[5]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full health with Lipsey and Jefferson at 100%. Coach **T.J. Otzelberger** has strong March experience (Sweet 16 in 2024, Elite Eight run in 2025). Iowa State historically excels in the NCAA Tournament, reaching **Sweet 16 five times since 2012** (most recent 2024) with a 20-12 record under Otzelberger.[4][5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Freshman Dominykas Pleta (6'11\" F)** stepping up in rim protection and pick-and-roll finishing; his 7.2 points and 5.8 rebounds off the bench could swing close games if he exploits slower bigs in March.[1][2]",
