      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n**Malik Thomas (G, 6'5\" Graduate Student, 18.2 PPG)** leads the Cavaliers as their primary scorer and playmaker, dangerous with his quick first step, mid-range pull-ups, and ability to create off the dribble against slower defenders[1][2][3].  \n**Ugonna Onyenso (C, 7'0\" Sr, 12.4 PPG, 9.1 RPG)** dominates the paint as a rim protector and rebounder, excelling in pick-and-roll finishes and alley-oop lobs while altering shots at a 15% block rate[1][2][3].  \n**Devin Tillis (F, 6'7\" Graduate Student, 11.8 PPG)** provides versatile scoring inside and out, lethal in transition and on second-chance opportunities with his physicality and 38% three-point shooting[1][3].\n\n## Team Identity\nVirginia plays at a deliberate **pack-line defense** tempo (62.3 possessions per game), emphasizing suffocating man-to-man pressure that forces turnovers (18.2% TO rate) and limits paint scoring, rooted in Tony Bennett's philosophy now adapted by interim coach Griff Aldrich[5]. Offensively, they run a motion-based scheme focused on high-post entries, cutting, and selective three-point shooting (34.2% on 19.1 attempts per game), with average rebounding strength led by Onyenso's double-doubles[1][5].\n\n## Recent Form\nOver the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), Virginia went 9-1, including a seven-game winning streak capped by an **ACC Tournament semifinal win over Duke (72-65) before falling to North Carolina in the final (68-74)**, showcasing defensive stands in crunch time like holding Duke to 39% FG[5]. Their only loss was a neutral-site thriller to Kansas, but they've won eight straight road/neutral games by double digits.\n\n## Strengths\nVirginia's elite **defensive efficiency (99.1)** smothers opponents, particularly in half-court sets where they rank top-5 nationally in opponent FG% (41.2%) and paint points allowed (38.4 per game), favoring them against inefficient shooters or teams lacking elite bigs[query]. They excel in matchups vs. up-tempo guards (e.g., forcing 22% TO rate) and mid-majors, as seen in their 29-5 record with 15 double-digit wins.\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerabilities emerge against **high-powered three-point offenses** (opponents shooting 37% from deep in losses) and athletic wings who stretch their deliberate pace, as smaller lineups struggle on the glass (29.8 RPG allowed to top rebounders)[1][5]. Teams like fast-breaking Big 12 squads give them trouble, evident in their two non-conference losses.\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported; Onyenso is fully healthy post-ankle tweak in February[5]. Coach **Griff Aldrich** (1st season as interim after Bennett's retirement) has zero NCAA head coaching experience but leverages strong assistant input[5]. Virginia boasts **strong March Madness history** (7 Elite Eights, 2019 championship), thriving as seeds with top-20 defenses (12-3 tourney record since 2014).\n\n## X-Factor\n**Martin Carrere's (G/F, 6'8\" Fr)** breakout from the bench—if he sustains his 42% three-point shooting on 4.2 attempts in March, it spaces the floor for Thomas and Tillis drives, potentially elevating their ceiling against switchable defenses[1][3][5].",
