      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nTexas Tech's top three players are **Christian Anderson (G, sophomore)**, averaging 18.4 points per game (586 points in 1189 minutes), **JT Toppin (F, junior)**, averaging 17.2 points and leading with 8.5 rebounds per game (271 rebounds), and **Donovan Atwell (G, senior)**, averaging 13.5 points with elite 3-point shooting (124/273, 45.4%). Anderson is dangerous as a high-volume playmaker with 236 assists and 105 threes, excelling in transition and pick-and-rolls[1][3]. Toppin dominates inside-out with 43 blocks and physicality in the paint, making him a mismatch nightmare[1]. Atwell's sharpshooting stretches defenses, forcing help and opening lanes[1].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nTexas Tech plays at a deliberate **tempo (67.8 possessions, 256th nationally)**, emphasizing a pack-line **defensive philosophy** that holds opponents to 105.0 adjusted efficiency while forcing turnovers (12.78% opponent turnover rate)[5]. Offensively, they run a motion scheme with heavy **three-point volume (48.03% of FGA, 25th nationally; 39.34% 3P%, 5th)** and strong 2-point efficiency (53.60%)[5]. **Rebounding** is a strength on the offensive glass (33.27% ORB%, 85th), led by forwards like Toppin and LeJuan Watts[5].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month leading to the Big 12 Tournament, Texas Tech went 5-3, including a three-game winning streak snapped by a loss where LeJuan Watts was banged up[5][6]. They advanced to the Big 12 semifinals before falling, showcasing improved shooting (39% from three in tournament games) but struggling with free-throw defense (opponents 69.41% FT)[1][5].\n\n## Strengths\n\nTexas Tech excels in **perimeter shooting** (top-5 3P%) and **defensive efficiency** (105.0 AdjD, elite at rim protection with 43 blocks from Toppin)[1][5]. They dominate slower-paced matchups, forcing misses (49.43% opponent eFG%, 82nd) and crashing boards, favoring them against inefficient shooters or teams lacking size[5].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **high-tempo teams** that exploit their slower pace and poor FTA/FGA (27.09%, 351st), leading to foul trouble[5]. Weak free-throw shooting (71.46%) and average defensive rebounding (30.09%, 165th) hurt against offensive-rebounding squads[5].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\n**Christian Anderson** (groin) is fully available after returning March 14; **LeJuan Watts (F)** is banged up but probable (14.5 PPG, 186 rebounds)[5][6]. Coach Grant McCasland (3rd season) has limited tournament experience but guided solid regular seasons[4]. Texas Tech's March Madness history includes a 2019 Final Four run under past coaches, but recent seeds average #6 with early exits[4].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Donovan Atwell's three-point shooting**—if he sustains 45% volume (124 makes), it unlocks Anderson/Toppin; cold streaks expose interior reliance[1].",
