      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\nTennessee's top three players are **Ja'Kobi Gillespie (Sr. G, 16.4 PPG)**, **Felix Okpara (F, 19.2 PPG)**, and **Nate Ament (Fr. F, 9.4 PPG)**.[1][2][4]\n\n- **Ja'Kobi Gillespie** leads the team with elite playmaking (5.5 APG) and scoring efficiency (159/393 FG, 42/127 3PT), making him dangerous as a quick guard who exploits defenses with drives, pull-up threes, and 68 steals for transition opportunities.[1][2]\n- **Felix Okpara** dominates inside with 200/492 FG and 88 threes, using his length for lobs, putbacks (76 ORB), and shot-blocking (team-high potential), while his 594 points highlight scoring versatility.[1][4]\n- **Nate Ament** provides rebounding prowess (205 total, 6.6 RPG) and efficiency (124/202 FG), dangerous on the glass (57 ORB) and cutting for easy buckets, stretching floors minimally but crashing effectively.[1][4]\n\n## Team Identity\nTennessee plays at a **controlled tempo** with an elite man-to-man defensive philosophy under Rick Barnes, holding opponents to 100.0 adjusted defensive efficiency through physicality (119 team blocks, 248 steals).[1][4] Offensively, they run a **gap-attack scheme** emphasizing paint touches and rebounding (1405 total rebounds, 529 ORB), with solid three-point volume (214/640) but strength in second-chance points rather than elite outside shooting.[1]\n\n## Recent Form\nIn the last month leading to Selection Sunday, Tennessee went 5-3, snapping a three-game skid with wins over mid-tier SEC foes but stumbling against top guards in losses.[5] They entered the SEC Tournament as a 4-seed, beating Rice 91-66 (Okpara 20 pts) in quarters but falling in semis to a higher seed, finishing 22-11 overall (11-7 SEC).[1][5]\n\n## Strengths\nTennessee excels in **defensive rebounding (876 DRB)** and physical frontcourt play, forcing turnovers (opponents' 3.9 BPG allowed but Tennessee's 3.6 BPG limits second chances).[1][4] They favor matchups against slower, perimeter-heavy teams, where their length (Ament/Okpara/Carey combine for 602 REB) and efficiency (114.6 adj. off.) overwhelms in the paint.[1][4]\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable to **quick guard play and three-point barrages**, as their 100.0 adj. def. struggles against elite shooters (opponents shoot 33.6% from three in losses).[1][4] Teams with superior backcourt speed and spacing, like those mimicking Maryland's style (former Gillespie home), exploit their deliberate tempo and occasional turnover issues (385 team TO).[1]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported; full rotation healthy including key transfers like Gillespie and Carey.[2][4] Coach **Rick Barnes** brings vast experience (11th Tennessee season, multiple Sweet 16s, 263-120 record).[5] Program history shines with consistent NCAA bids (8 straight), two Elite Eights (2010, 2024), but just one Final Four (2024 runners-up).[5]\n\n## X-Factor\n**Ja'Kobi Gillespie's three-point stroke (42/127)**—if he heats up (33% clip), Tennessee's offense surges to upset potential; cold nights expose their halfcourt stagnation.[1][2]",
