      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Somtochukwu \"Somto\" Cyril (C, Sophomore, 6-11, 260 lbs)** averages 14.2 points, 8.7 rebounds, and 2.1 blocks per game, making him dangerous with his physicality in the paint, elite rim protection, and ability to finish through contact on lobs and putbacks[1][2][3].  \n**Kanon Catchings (F, Sophomore, 6-9, 220 lbs)** contributes 12.8 points and 6.4 rebounds, excelling as a versatile scorer who stretches the floor with 38% three-point shooting and attacks closeouts aggressively[1][2][4].  \n**Jordan Ross (G, Junior, 6-3, 195 lbs)** leads the backcourt at 11.5 points and 4.2 assists, dangerous for his quickness in transition, crafty drives, and 42% three-point accuracy that punishes overhelping defenses[2][3][4].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nGeorgia plays at a deliberate **mid-tempo** (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** under coach Mike White that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent turnover rate) and limits paint scoring. Offensively, they run a **gap-drive scheme** with heavy reliance on **three-point shooting** (36.8% as a team, 22.5 attempts per game) and dominant **rebounding strength** (39.1 rebounds per game, top-25 nationally) led by their frontcourt size[1][3].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 17-March 17), Georgia went 7-3, including a five-game winning streak to close the regular season before a **semifinal loss in the SEC Tournament** to Alabama (78-72). Key wins: road at Kentucky (81-76) and home vs. Tennessee (89-84), showcasing improved three-point shooting (41% over the stretch)[1][3].\n\n## Strengths\n\nGeorgia excels in **interior dominance** and rebounding, outrebounding opponents by +6.2 per game, which fuels second-chance points (15.4 per game). Their **defensive efficiency (104.5)** smothers mid-majors and teams lacking size. Matchups favor them against guard-heavy or slow-paced teams like Big 12 squads, where Cyril and 7-1 freshman Jackson McVey overwhelm the glass[1][2][3].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **perimeter-oriented teams** that exploit their average guard defense (opponents shoot 35.2% from three), as undersized backcourt players like 5-11 Marcus \"Smurf\" Millender struggle against taller wings. Fast-paced, athletic squads (e.g., Big Ten transition teams) trouble them, forcing 14.8 turnovers per game in losses[1][4].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full health entering the #8 vs. #9 first-round matchup. Coach **Mike White** brings strong tournament experience (Florida Sweet 16 in 2017, Elite Eight in 2014; 5-4 NCAA record at Georgia). Program history is modest (last Sweet 16: 2008; 3-6 as 8-seed since 1985), but this roster's size gives upset potential[1][3][5].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Three-point shooting variance**: Georgia's 118.5 adjusted offensive efficiency surges (125+) when hitting 35%+ from deep but craters against zones if cold (under 30%), potentially deciding early-round survival[1][3].",
