      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Tavari Johnson (G, Sr.)**: Leads the team with 18.4 PPG (251-490 FG, 684 total points in 1087 minutes), excelling as a high-volume scorer with elite playmaking (170 assists) and 75 threes made; dangerous due to his scoring efficiency and ability to create off the dribble against slower defenders.[1][4]\n\n**Amani Lyles (F, Sr.)**: Second-leading scorer at 13.3 PPG (181-325 FG, 495 points), dominates the glass with 273 rebounds (76 offensive) and provides rim protection (24 blocks); his physicality and second-chance opportunities make him a matchup nightmare for undersized fronts.[1][4]\n\n**Shammah Scott (G, Sr.)**: Contributes 11.7 PPG (153-341 FG, 433 points) with prolific three-point shooting (81 makes on 192 attempts) and 100 assists; his perimeter threat and vision stretch defenses, forcing help rotations he exploits in transition.[1][4]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nAkron plays at a controlled tempo, emphasizing **defensive efficiency (103.1)** with a pack-line philosophy that forces turnovers and limits paint penetration, anchored by length from wings like Evan Mahaffey (57 steals).[1][4] Offensively, they run a motion scheme (adj. offensive efficiency 120.3) heavy on guard-driven pick-and-rolls, perimeter shooting (multiple 30%+ three-point shooters like Scott and Johnson), and strong rebounding (Lyles' 273 boards highlight second-chance prowess).[1][4]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month, Akron went 8-1, including a dominant **6-game winning streak** to close the regular season and a MAC Tournament championship run (semifinal win over Toledo 78-62, title game rout of Ohio 85-71). They averaged 82.3 PPG in tournament play, holding opponents under 65.[6]\n\n## Strengths\n\nAkron excels in **elite defense and rebounding**, suffocating opponents with length (Mahaffey and Lyles combine for 83 steals/blocks) and crashing the glass to fuel transition buckets; they thrive against inefficient mid-major or Big 12/SEC teams lacking size or perimeter D, as seen in MAC dominance (27-5 record).[1][4] Matchups favoring physical wings and slow-footed guards play to Johnson's scoring edge.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **elite three-point volume teams** that can match their tempo and spread the floor, exposing occasional lapses in closeouts (opponents shot 35% from deep in two losses). Small-ball lineups struggle against dominant bigs who pull Lyles away from the rim, as evidenced by a rare January loss to a taller Kent State squad.[6]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the Midwest #12 seed; full rotation healthy with seniors Johnson, Lyles, and Scott leading. Head coach John Groce brings proven tournament experience (Illinois Final Four in 2024, multiple NCAA berths); Akron's program history includes a Sweet 16 in 2005 but recent early exits (first-round losses 2010-2024), making this 27-5 squad their deepest run potential since.[6][7]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Evan Mahaffey's defensive versatility** (189 rebounds, 57 steals, 26 blocks): If the 6-6 Sr. wing locks down opposing stars and converts steals to fast breaks, Akron upsets; containment failures expose their backcourt.[1][4]",
