      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\nPurdue's top three players are **C.J. Cox** (Guard, Sophomore, 14.0 PPG), **Fletcher Loyer** (Guard, Senior, 13.8 PPG), and **Trey Kaufman-Renn** (Forward, 13.6 PPG).[1][3]  \n**C.J. Cox** averages 14.0 points on 44.3% FG and 36.4% from three (55 made), dangerous as a volume shooter with 151 three-point attempts and 84.8% FT.[3]  \n**Fletcher Loyer** scores 13.8 PPG with elite 69.8% eFG, hitting 98 threes on 233 attempts, excelling as a sharpshooting guard who stretches defenses.[1]  \n**Trey Kaufman-Renn** posts 13.6 PPG and leads with 8.5 RPG (97 ORB, 183 DRB), a rebounding force who dominates inside with physicality.[1][3]\n\n## Team Identity\nPurdue plays at a deliberate **tempo** (pace 65.9, 334th nationally), emphasizing half-court execution with a **defensive philosophy** anchored by length and rebounding (26.19% def orb allowed, top-25).[8] Offensively, they run a **scheme** built on efficient shooting (57.66% eFG, 11th nationally) and inside-out play, ranking high in **three-point shooting** volume while dominating **rebounding** (36.67% off reb, 20th).[8]\n\n## Recent Form\nIn the last month leading to the Big Ten Tournament, Purdue went 8-2, including a five-game winning streak snapped by a conference semifinal loss.[9] They finished conference play 13-7 and exited the **Big Ten Tournament** in the semifinals after a strong quarterfinal win, carrying momentum into the NCAA field with their 27-8 record.[9]\n\n## Strengths\nPurdue excels in **rebounding** (led by Kaufman-Renn's 8.5 RPG and Cluff's 7.4), **shooting efficiency** (AdjOE 123.0), and **defense** (AdjDE 105.7, low turnover% at 11.85).[1][3][8] They dominate **matchups** against smaller, perimeter-oriented teams, using size like 7-4 Daniel Jacobsen (44 blocks, 1.3 BPG) and Oscar Cluff (28 blocks) to control paint and glass.[3]\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable to **up-tempo, athletic teams** that push pace and attack their deliberate style, exposing average steals (e.g., Braden Smith's 1.8 SPG as outlier) and occasional turnover issues (14.02% def TO%).[8] Perimeter defense can falter against elite guards, as their 52.28% opponent eFG indicates gaps beyond rebounding.[8]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major **injuries** reported entering the tournament; full roster available including key returners like Cox, Loyer, and Smith.[4] Coach **Matt Painter** brings deep **tournament experience** (21st season, multiple Sweet 16s). Purdue's **historical March Madness success** includes a 2024 Final Four run, but recent early exits highlight seeding volatility despite strong regular seasons.[9]\n\n## X-Factor\n**Fletcher Loyer's three-point shooting** (98 makes on 233 attempts); if he sustains 42% volume from deep, Purdue's spacing unlocks their bigs for a deep run—cold streaks have plagued past tourneys.[1]",
