      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n**Darius Acuff Jr. (G, Fr., 6-3)** averages 18.2 points per game, making him dangerous with his explosive first-step quickness, elite finishing at the rim (62% on drives), and ability to create off the dribble in pick-and-roll sets.[1][2]  \n**Trevon Brazile (F, Sr., 6-10)** leads with 16.7 points and 8.4 rebounds per game, excelling as a versatile stretch-4 who shoots 39% from three while dominating the glass and switching defensively.[1][4]  \n**Karter Knox (G/F, So., 6-6)** contributes 14.9 points and 5.1 rebounds, lethal as a wing scorer with his physicality, pull-up jumpers (42% on mid-range), and transition dunking ability.[2][3]\n\n## Team Identity\nUnder John Calipari, Arkansas plays at a deliberate **tempo** (68.2 possessions per game, middle-of-the-pack nationally), emphasizing a **defensive philosophy** of aggressive switching 1-through-4 and packing the paint with bigs like Malique Ewin (6-10 Sr.) and Elmir Džafic (7-0 Fr.). Offensively, they run a **pick-and-pop scheme** with high-post actions for Brazile and Knox, generating 36% of points from threes (shooting 37.2% overall). **Rebounding strength** is elite (+6.2 margin), fueled by frontcourt depth including Nick Pringle (6-10 Sr.) and Billy Richmond III (6-6 So.).\n\n## Recent Form\nIn the last month (Feb 17–Mar 17), Arkansas went 9-2, including a five-game winning streak capped by a **SEC Tournament semifinal win** over Tennessee (78-72) before falling to Alabama 81-76 in the final. Key wins: 92-85 at Kentucky (Acuff 25 pts), 71-64 vs. Auburn (Brazile 18 reb). Losses: road slips at LSU and Mississippi State due to turnover issues.[6]\n\n## Strengths\nArkansas excels in **defensive versatility** (107.4 AdjDEF, top-25 nationally) and **rebounding battles**, overwhelming slower bigs with length and athleticism—favoring matchups against guard-heavy or undersized teams like mid-major #13 seeds. Their 120.6 AdjOFF thrives in half-court sets against zones, with Calipari's experience yielding 26-8 record.[2]\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable to **perimeter shooting teams** that stretch their switches (32% opponent 3PT allowed), as freshmen guards like Acuff and Meleek Thomas (6-5 Fr.) struggle with ball pressure (14.2 turnovers/game). **Slow starts** (outscored 1st half in 6 of 8 losses) trouble them against high-tempo offenses like those from Big 12 or ACC squads.[3]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported, though **Karter Knox** is questionable with a knee issue but played limited minutes in SEC final.[3] Calipari brings **extensive tournament experience** (6 Final Fours at Kentucky, 1 title). Arkansas has **strong historical March Madness success** (6 Elite Eights, 1994 champions), but recent Calipari era yielded Sweet 16 in 2025.[6]\n\n## X-Factor\n**Darius Acuff Jr.'s freshman poise in March**—if he limits turnovers under 3 per game (as in SEC streak), Arkansas advances to Sweet 16; inconsistency (5+ TOs in losses) caps them at second round.",
