      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nHawaii's top three players are senior center **Isaac Johnson** (7-0, 240 lbs), averaging 14.2 points and 9.1 rebounds per game as an All-Big West First Team selection, dangerous with his rim protection, putback dunks, and 62% field goal efficiency inside the arc[1][3][6]. Graduate guard **Dre Bullock** (6-6, 190 lbs), All-Big West Second Team, scores 13.8 points per game with elite perimeter shooting (38% from three on 6.2 attempts), thriving in pick-and-rolls and off-ball movement[1][3]. Senior forward **Gytis Nemeiksa** (6-8, 220 lbs) contributes 11.5 points and 7.3 rebounds, excelling as a stretch-four with 35% three-point shooting and versatile post scoring[2][6][7].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nHawaii plays at a deliberate tempo (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing a pack-line defensive philosophy that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent turnover rate) and elite rim protection led by their bigs[6]. Offensively, they run a motion scheme with high-low actions involving Johnson and wings like Bullock, ranking top-3 in Big West three-point volume (23.1 attempts per game at 36.2%) while dominating the glass (+4.8 rebound margin)[1][3][6]. Rebounding strength comes from their 6-10 frontcourt depth, but they prioritize efficiency over pace.\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17, 2026), Hawaii went 7-2, including a five-game winning streak capped by a 78-71 semifinal win over UC Irvine in the Big West Tournament before falling 82-76 to UC Santa Barbara in the championship game on March 15[3][6]. They averaged 82.1 points per game in that stretch, holding foes under 70 in six contests, with Johnson posting three double-doubles[1][3].\n\n## Strengths\n\nHawaii's **defensive efficiency** (94.9, top-40 nationally) shines against slower, paint-heavy teams, where Isaac Johnson's 2.8 blocks per game disrupt drives[1][6]. They excel in matchups favoring size mismatches, like versus guard-oriented mid-majors, outrebounding opponents by 5+ boards and converting 28% of opponent misses into second-chance points; their 36% three-point shooting punishes help rotations[3][6].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to explosive, transition-heavy teams that stretch their deliberate pace, as they rank bottom-100 in fast-break defense (1.12 points per possession allowed)[6]. Perimeter defense falters against elite shooters (opponents hit 35.8% from three), and thin guard depth exposes them to full-court pressure, leading to 14.2 turnovers per game in losses[1][2].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the tournament; full rotation healthy[1][2]. Head coach Eran Ganot (11th year) has solid tournament experience, leading Hawaii to three NCAA bids (2015-16 First Four win, 2022 First Round exit, 2024 First Four loss), with a 1-3 all-time March Madness record[6]. Program history is limited, with just four appearances since 1986 and no advancement past the First Round.\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Dre Bullock's three-point volume**—if he sustains 38%+ on high attempts against West Region athleticism, Hawaii's spacing unlocks Johnson inside; cold nights (under 30%) doom their half-court offense[3].",
