      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Chris Ashby (SR G, 6'2\", 19.8 PPG)**: Preseason All-ASUN unanimous selection, Ashby is the Royals' primary scorer and playmaker, excelling in perimeter shooting and driving to the basket, making him dangerous against slower defenders with his quick first step and 38% three-point shooting.[1][6]\n\n**Carson Schwieger (SO F, 6'8\", 15.2 PPG)**: Versatile forward who dominates inside-out, using his length for rebounds and transition scoring; his ability to stretch the floor with mid-range jumpers poses matchup problems for smaller bigs.[1][3]\n\n**Nasir Mann (SR G, 6'5\", 13.4 PPG)**: Senior leader who thrives in pick-and-roll sets, dangerous as a slasher and spot-up shooter, averaging 4.2 assists per game while disrupting passing lanes on defense.[1][6]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nQueens plays at a moderate tempo (68.5 possessions per game), emphasizing balanced half-court offense through high-low actions involving forwards like Schwieger and Gus Larson (6'10\" SR F). Defensively, they employ a pack-line philosophy under coach Grant Leonard, focusing on help rotations to protect the rim (114.4 AdjDEF). Offensively, they run flex motion with heavy three-point reliance (36% of shots, 35.2% accuracy), but rebounding is a weakness (34.2 RPG, bottom-third in ASUN).[1][6]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (Feb 17–Mar 17, 2026), Queens went 7-3, including a five-game winning streak capped by a 78-72 semifinal win over Florida Gulf Coast in the ASUN Tournament before falling 85-79 to top-seeded Stetson in the championship game. Key wins: 92-88 at Lipscomb (Feb 22), 76-71 vs. Kennesaw State (Mar 8).[6]\n\n## Strengths\n\nQueens excels in perimeter shooting (top-5 ASUN in 3PT%), forcing turnovers (15.8% opponent TO rate), and transition scoring off misses. They favor matchups against slow, perimeter-weak teams, using Ashby's guard play and length from wings like Reid Celestin (JR G, 6'6\") to exploit switches.[1][6]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable on the boards (weak offensive rebounding at 24.5%), allowing second-chance points, and in the paint against physical post players (opponents score 52.1 PPG inside). Athletic, rebounding-heavy teams like mid-majors with strong bigs give them trouble, as seen in non-con losses to UNC Greensboro.[6]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available, including key returners Ashby and Mann. Coach Grant Leonard (4th year) has zero NCAA Tournament experience, guiding Queens in their 3rd D1 season. Program has no prior March Madness appearances, entering as a true Cinderella #15 seed with 20-13 record after transitioning from D2.[1][6]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Chris Ashby's three-point shooting**: If Ashby heats up from deep (career 37% in big games), Queens can upset by stretching defenses; cold nights expose their interior scoring limits.[1][6]",
