      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Tamin Lipsey (Sr. G, 6-1)**: Averaging 14.7 PPG (411 points in 981 minutes), Lipsey is the floor general with 156 assists and 68 steals, making him dangerous as a quick, disruptive defender who penetrates and creates chaos in transition[4][1].\n\n**Milan Momcilovic (Jr. F, 6-8)**: Leading scorer at 21.0 PPG (581 points in 1024 minutes) on elite 33% three-point shooting (127/256), his sharpshooting stretch forward ability opens the floor and punishes sagging defenses[4][2].\n\n**Joshua Jefferson (Sr. F, 6-9)**: Posts 20.6 PPG (573 points in 1080 minutes) with 259 rebounds (66 offensive) and 167 assists, excelling as a versatile scorer/rebounder who attacks the rim and facilitates from the post[4][3].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nIowa State plays at a controlled tempo, emphasizing **elite man-to-man defense** (90.2 adjusted efficiency) with aggressive trapping and switching to force turnovers. Offensively, they run a motion scheme (110.5 adjusted efficiency) heavy on **high-volume three-point shooting** (led by Momcilovic) and cutting, supported by strong **offensive rebounding** from bigs like Jefferson and Buchanan (193 rebounds)[4].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 17-March 17, 2026), Iowa State went 8-2, including a five-game winning streak capped by a Big 12 Tournament semifinal win before falling in the final to Houston. They averaged 78 PPG while holding opponents under 60, showcasing defensive dominance in knockout play[4].\n\n## Strengths\n\nThey excel in **turnover-forcing defense** and **offensive rebounding**, winning the paint battle against slower bigs. Matchups favor them versus inefficient shooting teams or those lacking perimeter quickness, as in their Big 12 wins over Kansas and Baylor[4].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **athletic, switchable defenses** that neutralize their three-point volume and limit second-chance points. Teams with elite rim protection and guard depth, like Duke or Gonzaga, trouble them by exposing limited size beyond Jefferson[4].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available with Lipsey and Jefferson at 100%. Coach T.J. Otzelberger brings strong tournament experience (Sweet 16 in 2024, Elite Eight in 2025). Iowa State has March Madness success with four Sweet 16s since 2012, including a 2024 run[3][5].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Blake Buchanan's (Jr. F) rim protection and rebounding** (287 points, 193 rebounds, 32 blocks); if he dominates the glass against physical fronts in Fort Worth, Iowa State advances deep as an 8-seed[4].",
