      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\nSyracuse's top scorers are **Naithan George** (PG, Jr., 10.9 PPG), who excels as a crafty playmaker with 174 assists and 1.5 SPG, dangerous in transition and drawing fouls at 83.3% FT[1][2][6]. **JJ Starling** (G, Sr., 10.9 PPG) is a versatile scorer at 41.9% FG, lethal with his size (6-4) in driving and mid-range pull-ups, backed by 72 assists and 26 steals[6]. **Donnie Freeman** (PF, So., 16.5 PPG) dominates inside at 47.4% FG and leads with 7.2 RPG (23 OREB), a mismatch nightmare with his scoring touch and shot-blocking (21 BLK)[1][2][6].\n\n## Team Identity\nSyracuse plays at a deliberate tempo under Adrian Autry, emphasizing **2-3 zone defense** (elite 83.9 AdjDEF, holding foes to 74.3 PPG)[1][2]. Offensively, they run a structured half-court scheme (98.5 AdjOFF) with heavy rebounding (34.1 RPG, 40.3 opp DRPG allowed) via bigs like William Kyle III (7.1 RPG, 2.5 BPG)[2][6]. They shoot 32.9% from three (229 makes) but rely more on paint dominance and second-chance points (328 OREB team)[6].\n\n## Recent Form\nIn the last month leading to the ACC Tournament, Syracuse went 7-3, including a 5-game win streak snapped by a conference semifinal loss to a top seed. They finished ACC play 15-17 overall but peaked with road wins over Miami and NC State, averaging 82 PPG in those[4]. In the **ACC Tournament**, they reached the quarterfinals before falling, securing their #9 seed auto-bid via regular-season momentum[4].\n\n## Strengths\nSyracuse thrives in **rebounding battles** (1061 total REB, led by Kyle's 226 and Freeman's 166) and rim protection (189 team BLK, 5.9/game), smothering paint-heavy teams with their zone[1][2][6]. They favor slower, physical matchups in the Fort Worth region, where their efficiency edges (top-25 defense) exploit sloppy offenses—ideal vs. mid-majors or inefficient shooters.\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable to **perimeter shooting teams**, as opponents hit 34.1% from three against the zone, and Syracuse turns it over 10.7/game (342 TO)[1][6]. Big, athletic wings trouble them by stretching the zone; high-tempo squads (e.g., those pushing 75+ possessions) expose their 46.5% FG and modest 32.9% 3PT[6].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported entering the tournament; full rotation healthy with George and Starling at 32+ MPG[6]. Coach **Adrian Autry** (3rd year, 23-8 record) brings solid experience as Jim Boeheim's longtime assistant, including Syracuse's 2003 title run[4]. Historically, Syracuse boasts **6 Final Fours** (last 2016) and 11 Elite Eights as a tournament juggernaut, often excelling as double-digit seeds with zone magic[4].\n\n## X-Factor\n**William Kyle III's** rim protection (80 BLK, 2.5 BPG)—if he anchors the zone vs. slashers in Fort Worth, Syracuse advances; foul trouble could unravel their defense[2].",
