      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Braden Sparks (G, Sr., 17.6 PPG)** leads the Stags in scoring, assists (2.6 APG), and steals (1.2 SPG), making him dangerous as a quick guard who penetrates defenses and hits spot-up threes at a team-supporting 33.6% clip from deep[1][2].  \n**Brandon Benjamin (F, Fr., 10.3 RPG, 1.6 BPG)** dominates the glass with 10.4 total rebounds per game (333 total) and provides elite rim protection (51 blocks), excelling in transition and second-chance opportunities[1][2].  \n**Declan Wucherpfennig (G/F, Fr., 8.5 RPG)** is a versatile rebounder (288 total boards) with strong defensive metrics (105.8 DefRtg) and efficient shooting (57.8 TS%), posing matchup problems as a 6-7 forward who crashes boards and switches seamlessly[1][5].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nFairfield plays at a controlled tempo emphasizing elite **defensive efficiency (79.5)**, suffocating opponents with physical rebounding (38.8 RPG, +4.3 margin) led by Benjamin and Wucherpfennig[3]. Offensively, they run a balanced scheme (107.6 AdjOEff) with heavy three-point volume (258 made at 33.6%) via Sparks' drive-and-kick, complemented by strong free-throw shooting (73.4%) and interior finishing from Munkadi (56.5% FG)[1][3].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month, Fairfield has surged with a 10-1 streak, including a dominant MAAC Tournament championship run where they beat top seeds by holding foes under 60 points in wins over Iona and Rider[3]. Their only loss was a neutral-site tune-up, but they've won 8 straight entering the NCAA field, averaging 82 PPG while allowing 62[1].\n\n## Strengths\n\nFairfield excels in **rebounding dominance** (top in MAAC) and **defensive pressure**, forcing turnovers (Sparks' 1.4 SPG) and protecting the rim (Benjamin's 1.6 BPG), ideal against sloppy or perimeter-heavy teams[1][2]. They favor matchups with slower bigs, where Benjamin and Wucherpfennig overwhelm the glass, and mid-major foes lacking elite athleticism.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **explosive guard play**, as their backcourt (Mejia, Rawlins) can struggle containing elite athletes, yielding open threes (opponents shoot 34.0% from deep)[3]. Physical, half-court grinders with strong interior scoring trouble them, exposing occasional offensive stagnation beyond Sparks (team 11.9 APG)[3].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available with Benjamin and Sparks at 100%[4]. Coach Chris Perkins brings solid experience (2 Sweet 16s at prior stops), though Fairfield's program history is limited (last NCAA bid 1998, 0-1 all-time)[7]. As a #11 seed, their efficiency edges position them for an upset in Fort Worth.\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Braden Sparks' three-point volume**—if he sustains 33%+ on high attempts amid tournament pressure, Fairfield's spacing unlocks Benjamin inside; cold nights doom their offense[1][2].",
