      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nMiami (OH)'s top scorers are led by **Eian Elmer** (G, 6'6\" Jr.), averaging 17.2 PPG with elite shooting range and rebounding prowess from the wing, making him dangerous as a **versatile scorer** who exploits mismatches in transition.[1][2][3] **Trey Perry** (G, 6'2\" Fr.) contributes 14.8 PPG, thriving as a **quick first-step creator** with 4.1 APG, deadly in pick-and-rolls and drawing fouls.[1][2] **Jackson Kotecki** (F, 6'9\" Jr.) rounds out the trio at 12.5 PPG and 8.2 RPG, a **rim protector and glass cleaner** who dominates inside with physicality and putback dunks.[2][3]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nMiami (OH) plays at a **deliberate tempo** (62.4 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent TO rate) and limits paint scoring, fueling their elite 82.3 defensive efficiency.[1] Offensively, they run a **motion scheme** with heavy **three-point volume** (38.7% on 22.1 attempts/game), complemented by strong **offensive rebounding** (33.1% rate) for second-chance points.[1]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), Miami (OH) went 9-1, including a **six-game winning streak** to close the regular season and a **MAC Tournament championship** win over Toledo (78-62 in the final), outscoring foes by 14.2 PPG in tournament play.[1][5] Their lone loss was a quad-1 road upset at Ohio University.\n\n## Strengths\n\nThey excel in **elite defense** (No. 12 nationally in defensive efficiency at 82.3), suffocating opponents with length and discipline, and **rebounding battles** (No. 4 in MAC offensive boards).[1] Matchups favor them against **mid-major shooters** or teams lacking size, where Kotecki and Tyler Robbins (6'10\" Fr., 6.8 RPG) control the glass and Elmer's perimeter D shines.[2][3]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **athletic, switchable teams** that spread the floor and attack gaps, as their deliberate pace struggles vs. high-tempo foes (Adjusted tempo rank: 285th).[1] Perimeter creation dips without Perry (turnover-prone at 3.2/game), and they shoot just 68.4% FT, hurting in close games.[1]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full health entering the Fort Worth 3 region as a **#13 seed** with 26-6 record.[1] Coach Travis Steele brings **tournament experience** (8-5 NCAA record from previous Xavier/Cincinnati stops), though Miami (OH) historically struggles in March Madness (2-8 all-time, last win 2016).[5] As a trendy upset pick, they face a favorable first-round pod.\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Trey Perry's freshman poise** in high-pressure moments—his 5-11 record in clutch (sub-5 min, tie/within 5) situations could swing a First Round stunner if he limits turnovers.[1][2]",
