      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nVermont's top scorers are led by **Gus Yalden**, a forward averaging **16.6 points per game**, dangerous due to his versatile scoring inside and ability to stretch defenses with mid-range pull-ups.[7]  \n**T.J. Long** (listed as TJ Long in stats), a guard/forward averaging **12.1 points per game** with strong rebounding (4.8 per game), excels in transition and spot-up shooting, making him a mismatch threat on the wing.[1][2]  \n**Sean Blake**, a guard averaging **4.0 assists per game** while contributing offensively, is lethal as a playmaker who penetrates and finds cutters, with **T.J. Hurley** adding **0.8 steals per game** as a pesky on-ball defender.[7]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nVermont plays at a controlled tempo, prioritizing **elite defense** (adjusted defensive efficiency of 82.3) with a pack-line philosophy that forces turnovers and limits second-chance points, holding opponents to 68.5 points per game.[1] Offensively, they run a motion scheme emphasizing **three-point shooting** (34.5% as a team) and rebounding dominance (+3.6 margin), scoring 73.4 points per game at an adjusted offensive efficiency of 99.0.[1][2]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nEntering the tournament, Vermont holds a strong **25-7 record**, winning their last 8 of 10 games including a gritty **84-89 double-overtime win** at Northeastern (11/15/25, though indicative of resilience in close contests).[1] They captured the **America East Conference Tournament title**, going undefeated in knockout rounds to secure the automatic bid, with no major losing streak in the final month.[2]\n\n## Strengths\n\nVermont excels in **defensive efficiency** and **rebounding**, outrebounding foes by 3.6 per game (34.8 to 31.2), which fuels transition opportunities and suffocates slower teams.[2] They favor matchups against inefficient shooters or teams lacking size, where their pack-line defense and Yalden's presence disrupt rhythm.[1][7]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVermont struggles against high-tempo, athletic teams that exploit their moderate offensive efficiency (99.0 adjusted), as seen in overtime losses like 69-75 at Princeton (12/30/25) where they shot just 39.3%.[1] Poor free-throw shooting in stretches (e.g., 54.2% in a double-OT loss) and vulnerability to hot three-point shooting opponents expose them.[1]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available with **Gus Yalden** healthy as their star.[7] Head coach John Becker brings extensive tournament experience, with Vermont making 7 NCAA appearances in the last 10 years under him. Historically, the program has modest March Madness success (0-7 all-time record as of 2025), often bowing out early as a low seed but competitive in first rounds.[2]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Gus Yalden's scoring efficiency**—if he maintains 16.6 PPG against physical #3-seed frontcourts in the Fort Worth Region, Vermont can steal a first-round upset; inefficiency strands their defense-first identity.[7]",
