      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Jackson Hicke (G)**: Leads the team with 14.9 points per game and 2.4 assists per game. Dangerous due to his scoring versatility, playmaking ability, and efficiency as the primary ball-handler, creating open looks for teammates[2].\n\n**Malik Abdullahi (F, 6'7\")**: Averages 6.0 rebounds per game while contributing offensively inside. His length and athleticism make him a threat in the paint for putbacks, blocks, and transition plays[1][2].\n\n**Jack Stanton (G, 6'2\")**: Tops the team with 0.9 steals per game. Excels as a pesky on-ball defender who disrupts passing lanes, while providing quick scoring bursts off the bench[2].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nPrinceton plays at a deliberate tempo, emphasizing **elite defensive discipline** with a pack-line philosophy that forces turnovers and limits penetration (defensive efficiency of 87.2). Offensively, they run a motion scheme focused on ball movement and **high-volume three-point shooting**, exploiting cuts and screens. Rebounding is a relative strength via frontcourt size like Abdullahi and Jacob Huggins (0.6 blocks per game), but they prioritize spacing over crashing the glass aggressively[1][2].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month leading into the tournament, Princeton has surged with a **10-game winning streak**, including a dominant Ivy League Tournament championship run where they defeated Yale in the final 78-62. This hot streak erased earlier conference struggles (5-9 Ivy record), fueled by Hicke's scoring outbursts and stifling defense in knockout games[3].\n\n## Strengths\n\nPrinceton excels in **defensive efficiency (87.2)**, suffocating opponents with physicality and smart rotations, ideal against inefficient half-court offenses. They thrive in matchups favoring them like slower-paced teams or those reliant on mid-range shooting, where their three-point volume (complemented by adjusted offensive efficiency of 105.4) creates upset potential as a #9 seed[1][2].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **explosive athletic bigs** who dominate the glass and score in transition, exposing their lack of elite rim protection despite solid rebounding. Fast-paced, athletic teams that push tempo give them trouble, as seen in non-conference losses where they struggled to match physicality[4].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available including freshman Landon Clark (6'8\" G) providing wing depth[1]. Coach **Mitch Henderson** (14th season) brings solid tournament experience, reaching the Sweet 16 in 2023. Historically, Princeton has March Madness success as a mid-major, with a 2011 Sweet 16 run and recent upsets, positioning this 26-3 squad for a deep Sacramento 2 Region run[4].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Jackson Hicke's three-point shooting in clutch moments**: If he sustains 40%+ from deep against tournament pressure (building on his 14.9 PPG), Princeton can stun higher seeds; cold nights doom their spacing-dependent offense[2].",
