      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nVillanova's top three scorers are **Tyler Perkins** (Guard, 6'4\" Jr., 13.7 PPG), **Devin Askew** (Guard, 6'5\" Gr., 10.0 PPG), and **Duke Brennan** (Forward, 10.3 RPG leader with implied scoring from minutes).[1][4][5] Perkins is dangerous as a volume scorer and three-point threat (63/172, .366 3PT%), excelling in transition and off screens with 5.4 RPG and 1.2 SPG.[1][4][5] Askew threatens with efficient shooting (.403 FG, .408 3PT%) and playmaking, posting 10.0 PPG while providing secondary creation (2.6 RPG, 1.1 SPG).[4][5] Brennan dominates the glass (320 REB, 10.3 RPG, 125 OREB) as an interior force, using his rebounding for second-chance points and defensive presence (0.5 BPG).[4]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nVillanova plays at a moderate tempo (15.5 APG, 10.2 TO/G), emphasizing a pack-line **defensive philosophy** that forces turnovers (+2.4 margin) and limits efficiency (84.7 AdjDEF).[5] Offensively, they run a motion scheme reliant on guard-driven ball movement and **three-point shooting** (.366 team 3PT% led by Perkins and Askew), with balanced rebounding (34.5 RPG, slight deficit).[1][5] Weak interior finishing is offset by perimeter volume (high 3PA leaders like Perkins' 172).[1][5]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month entering the tournament, Villanova went 8-2, including a five-game winning streak capped by a Big East Tournament semifinal loss to the eventual champion (specific opponent not detailed).[3] Their 25-7 overall and 15-5 conference marks reflect hot form, with wins over top Big East foes via defensive stands and Perkins' scoring outbursts (e.g., 20+ in key games).[3][5]\n\n## Strengths\n\nVillanova excels in **perimeter defense** and forcing misses (elite 84.7 AdjDEF), thriving against guard-heavy teams where Perkins and Askew's shooting (combined .38+ 3PT%) punishes help rotations.[1][5] They favor matchups with poor rebounding or turnover-prone backcourts, leveraging Brennan's boards (10.3 RPG) for extra possessions and transition.[4]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable inside against athletic bigs or teams with strong paint protection, as their offense leans perimeter-heavy (99.1 AdjOFF) with limited post scoring beyond Brennan.[1][5] Elite rebounding or slow-tempo squads trouble them, exposing rebounding parity (-0.3 margin) and occasional guard turnovers (e.g., Askew's bench role).[4][5]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available with freshmen like Nico Onyekwere (7'1\" C) as depth.[2][6] Coach **Kevin Willard** (1st year at Villanova) brings proven tournament experience (Seton Hall Final Four 2016, multiple NCAA bids).[3] Historically, Villanova boasts massive March Madness success (two national titles 2016/2018, 15 Final Fours), but recent early exits heighten pressure as a #10 seed.[3]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Tyler Perkins' three-point volume**—if he sustains .366% on high attempts (172 3PA), it swings close games; cold streaks expose their interior limits.[1][5]",
