      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n**Connor Essegian (G, Sr., 6-4)** averages 14.2 points per game, dangerous as a sharpshooting transfer from Wisconsin who hits 38% of his threes on high volume, stretching defenses and igniting transition[1][2][3].  \n**Rienk Mast (F, Sr., 6-10)** leads with 12.8 points and 8.5 rebounds per game, a skilled post presence from Bradley with elite touch around the rim and rebounding instincts that dominate the glass[1][3][7].  \n**Kendall Blue (G, Sr., 6-6)** contributes 11.5 points and 4.2 assists per game, excelling as a versatile combo guard from St. Thomas who attacks seams, facilitates in pick-and-roll, and defends multiple positions[1][2][4].\n\n## Team Identity\nNebraska plays at a deliberate **tempo** (68.2 possessions per game), prioritizing half-court execution over run-and-gun. Their **defensive philosophy** is pack-line inspired, focusing on denying penetration and forcing contested jumpers, anchored by length inside (elite 92.5 adjusted defensive efficiency). Offensively, they run a **gap-attack scheme** with high-low actions involving bigs like Mast and Jarusevicius, moderate **three-point shooting** (35.2% on 19 attempts), and above-average **rebounding strength** (37.1 boards per game, top-40 nationally) via Mast and Buyuktuncel.\n\n## Recent Form\nIn the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), Nebraska went 6-4, including a three-game winning streak to close the regular season (wins over Northwestern, Minnesota, and Rutgers) before dropping two in the Big Ten Tournament. They earned a solid quarterfinal run, upsetting #6 Iowa 78-72 before falling to #2 Purdue 85-71 in the semis, showing resilience but struggling against elite size[1][7].\n\n## Strengths\nNebraska excels in **defensive rebounding** (72.4% rate) and rim protection, with Mast and 7-2 freshman Leo Curtis (6.2 blocks/40 minutes) smothering lobs. They thrive in **matchups** against guard-heavy or slow-paced teams, using switchable wings like Blue and Essegian to disrupt rhythm while their adjusted efficiencies (108.4 off, 92.5 def) suggest upset potential vs. mid-major or sloppy offenses.\n\n## Weaknesses\nTurnover creation is middling (14.2% opponent rate), leaving them vulnerable to ballhawks, and perimeter defense sags against elite shooters (36.8% opponent 3PT%). **Teams that give them trouble**: Fast, athletic squads with strong pick-and-roll bigs (e.g., Big Ten foes like Indiana) exploit their lack of elite quickness, as seen in losses to Ohio State and Illinois.\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported; Mast fully healthy after minor ankle tweak in February. Coach Fred Hoiberg brings strong **tournament experience** (three Sweet 16s as Iowa State head coach, NBA pedigree). Nebraska's **historical March Madness success** is limited (2-10 all-time, first tournament win since 1998), but this roster's battle-tested transfers signal breakout potential as a #11 seed in the Sacramento Region.\n\n## X-Factor\n**Rienk Mast's rebounding dominance**—if he grabs 12+ boards and limits second-chance points (Nebraska allows 9.8/game), their defense elevates to lock-down mode, fueling a potential run; otherwise, they get outmuscled by bracket heavyweights.",
