      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**AJ Lopez (G, Sr., 13.4 PPG)**: Lopez leads the team in scoring with 428 points on 131-280 FG and 41-104 from three, averaging 25.9 minutes per game. His **dangerous sharpshooting** (39.4% from deep) and playmaking (71 assists) make him a perimeter threat who can stretch defenses and create off the dribble[1][3].\n\n**Will Johnston (G, Sr., 9.7 PPG)**: Johnston contributes 311 points on 100-259 FG and 58-155 threes (37.4%), playing 27.5 MPG with 95 rebounds and 71 assists. His **versatile scoring** inside and out, plus elite rebounding for a guard, allows him to exploit mismatches and run the offense[3].\n\n**Mike Walz (C, Sr., ~7.8 PPG estimated from usage)**: Walz dominates the glass with 203 rebounds (6.5 RPG, 57 offensive), 14 steals, and 23 blocks across 659 minutes, anchoring the paint. His **rim protection and rebounding** make him dangerous in transition and second-chance opportunities[1][4].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nRichmond plays at a **controlled tempo** with an elite defensive focus (85.9 adjusted efficiency), emphasizing man-to-man pressure that forces turnovers (team-high steals and blocks). Offensively (105.7 adjusted efficiency), they run a **motion scheme heavy on three-point volume** (288 makes at 36.4%, 792 attempts), led by guards, while dominating rebounds (33.1 RPG, 1059 total) via Walz and forwards like Jaden Daughtry (92 rebounds, 3.0 RPG)[1][3][4].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (February-March 2026), Richmond went 8-2, including a **five-game winning streak** into the A-10 Tournament, capped by a championship win over VCU (semifinals: beat Dayton 72-65; final: 78-71). They avenged two regular-season losses to top the conference, with Lopez dropping 28 points in the title game[5].\n\n## Strengths\n\nRichmond excels in **elite defense and rebounding**, holding opponents to low efficiency while crashing the boards (277 offensive rebounds). They thrive against **mid-major or guard-heavy teams**, using Walz's paint presence and perimeter shooting (36.4% team 3P%) to control tempo and win low-possession games, as seen in their 87-54 rout of VMI[1][3][5].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **athletic, fast-paced teams** that attack the rim and limit threes, as their 75.3% FT rate and occasional turnover issues (311 total) expose them. Poor free-throw shooting from bigs like Daughtry (59.7%) hurts in close games against physical foes[1][3].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo reported injuries entering the tournament. Coach **Chris Mooney** (21st season) brings strong experience, with multiple A-10 titles and NCAA bids, including a 2011 Sweet 16 run. Historically, Richmond has **modest March success** (5 NCAA wins since 2000, last bid 2022 as #12 seed, lost First Round), but their 26-7 record positions them for an upset as a battle-tested #11[5].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**AJ Lopez's three-point volume**: If Lopez sustains 40%+ from deep (41 makes already), Richmond's spacing overwhelms bigger #6 seeds; cold nights doom their half-court offense[1][3].",
